# jellyfin-scan-music, jellyfin-image-backfill, music-library-sweep and
# lidarr-jellyfin-bridge all lived here. Jellyfin no longer has a music library:
# Navidrome owns music now, so all four targeted a library that does not exist.
# Left installed they would not have errored -- Jellyfin answers 204 to a refresh
# for a path under no library -- they would simply have done nothing, forever.
# Recover them from git history if music ever returns to Jellyfin.

# Every 10 min — tell an off-box dead-man's switch this host is alive. Exits 1
# (a reported condition, not a failure) when NAS_HEARTBEAT_URL is unset or when
# stack_watchdog has gone quiet, and 2 only when the ping itself cannot be
# delivered -- which cron_job.py then pushes, because a silently-failing
# heartbeat leaves a green dashboard elsewhere while this box is unmonitored.
*/10 * * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name heartbeat --max-age-min 40 -- python scripts/heartbeat.py >> logs/heartbeat.log 2>&1"
# */5 — re-assert qBittorrent's settings. Runs at the watchdog's cadence, not
# hourly, because it is now also the degraded-mode switch: if the CAKE shaper is
# missing it drops the upload cap 25 -> 15 Mbps, and an hour of unmanaged uplink
# is an hour of 5% packet loss. The upload cap is not cosmetic:
# set above the link's real upstream it fills the modem queue and costs 5% packet
# loss, which is what made remote Jellyfin playback stutter for weeks. The live
# value was observed drifting back to UNLIMITED on 2026-09-02 with the repo pin
# untouched, so pinning it in DESIRED_PREFS is necessary but not sufficient --
# something has to re-assert it. Idempotent: a correct stack logs "none".
*/5 * * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name qbit-settings-enforce --lock /tmp/nas-qbit-enforce.lock --max-age-min 30 -- python scripts/qbittorrent_settings_enforce.py >> logs/qbittorrent_settings_enforce.log 2>&1"
# :17 hourly — re-assert the CAKE shaper and the DSCP bulk marks. Neither is
# durable on its own: tc state is lost whenever the link goes down, and the
# iptables rules reference container IPs that Docker reassigns on recreate.
# Idempotent (apply tears down and rebuilds). systemd covers boot; this covers
# everything after it.
17 * * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name wan-shaper --lock /tmp/nas-wan-shaper.lock --max-age-min 180 -- sudo -n /home/tom/nas/scripts/wan_shaper.sh apply >> logs/wan_shaper.log 2>&1"
15 6 * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name verify-runtime --max-age-min 1560 --ok-codes 0 -- make verify-runtime VERIFY_NOTIFY=1 >> logs/verify_runtime.log 2>&1"
# PENDING-DESTINATION: uncomment once RESTIC_REPOSITORY is set in .env and
# restic is installed (sudo apt-get install -y restic). Until then this job
# would exit 2 nightly and ntfy every morning about a backup that was never
# configured. Verified in a cron-like environment (env -i, CWD=$HOME).
#0 2 * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c "cd /home/tom/nas && . .venv/bin/activate && python scripts/cron_job.py --name offsite-backup --lock /tmp/nas-offsite-backup.lock --max-age-min 2880 --fail-lane critical -- bash scripts/offsite_backup.sh --apply >> logs/offsite_backup.log 2>&1"
